      "text": "To solve the problem \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\), we will use the tangent addition formula and some trigonometric identities. The tangent addition formula states that:\n\\[\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}\n\\]\nWe need to express \\(\\tan 45^\\circ\\) in terms of \\(\\tan 20^\\circ\\) and \\(\\tan 25^\\circ\\). Notice that \\(\\tan 45^\\circ = 1\\), so we use the tangent addition formula with \\(A = 20^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 25^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\tan 45^\\circ = \\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\nSince \\(\\tan 45^\\circ = 1\\), we have:\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\\) gives:\n\\[\n1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[\n1 = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nWe can rewrite the left side as a single fraction:\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\nNow, let \\(x = \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\\). So we have:\n\\[\n1 = \\frac{x}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ}\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\\) gives:\n\\[\n1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = x\n\\]\nWe know that:\n\\[\n1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = \\tan 45^\\circ\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\n1 = \\tan 45^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\n\\]\nTherefore:\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = \\tan 45^\\circ = 1\n\\]\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
